From 25f1873ad4d47b3ef41a98e43582727ea8c42388 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:22:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] pool broker: re-key the child throttle off free VRAM onto
 prefill capacity
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A throttle keyed on free device VRAM stopped throttling, because the thing
free VRAM was a proxy for left the device.

Two changes on the serving side did it, in the same direction. A cutover moved
the KV pool to host RAM (451,264 -> 1,310,720 tokens; KV VRAM 5.6 -> 1.26 GB;
free VRAM 935-1,533 -> 6,463 MiB), and a bounded-gather fix put prefill
transients flat at 32 MiB at every context length. The rule free_mib // 560
then licensed ~11 concurrent children where it had licensed ~1, and two main
sessions were measured at 11-17 tok/s each.  [measured]

The gate is now the three resources that actually bind, each read live from
the backend's own numbers:

  (A) SLOTS. One slot held for every live main before a child is counted,
      plus a total cap of 2 streams. Derived from 6,436 warm decode-batch
      samples of the current build era, with cold-prefill-contended steps
      segmented out and post-restart warmup dropped: aggregate throughput
      rises monotonically 176.5 / 282.2 / 340.2 / 409.6 tok/s at concurrency
      1/2/3/4, so there is NO interior aggregate maximum below
      --max-running-requests 4 and the cap cannot be read off the aggregate
      curve at all. Keyed instead on a main lane's OWN rate, which retains
      100 / 80.0 / 64.2 / 58.0% of solo: 2 is the largest concurrency at
      which a lane keeps two thirds. This refuses the SECOND concurrent child
      outright, instead of letting six start and self-clamping once a 12-deep
      queue has appeared.  [measured]
  (B) PREFILL BACKLOG. num_waiting_uncached_tokens divided by the prefill
      rate the server reports about itself (total_prefill_uncached_tokens /
      total_prefill_busy_us = 8,481 tok/s), against the 30 s control-plane
      budget that is the judge timeout. Inside the remaining headroom each
      child is charged the eviction waste it causes, from the two measured
      anchors: 2,622 tokens with no contention, 114,385 with five other
      requests in flight (growth factor 2.145).  [measured]
  (C) POOL RESIDENCY. Backlog tokens charged against the pool before
      children. Correctly does not bind at 14-32% used.

Runtime free VRAM is demoted to a floor backstop that should almost never
fire: 560 MiB -- the largest allocation measured fatal, at a logged OOM --
plus one 32 MiB transient per scheduler slot = 688 MiB. It separates the three
geometries that matter (287 MiB critical; 935 and 6,463 MiB clear) and still
reads a runtime sample with a 90 s staleness fail-open, never the boot-time
available-memory figure.

At the live geometry that prompted this (588,268 uncached waiting = 69 s of
backlog, 2 mains, 3 running, 5,651 MiB free) the gate yields 0 child streams
where the old rule gave 10.  [measured] No main's window, growth ceiling or
request priority is touched by any of it: the share-bounded per-main grants
from patch 0003 are unchanged.

Every knob resolves at CALL time -- environment variable, then the broker's
own JSON config, then the module default -- so retuning no longer means
relaunching the sessions being throttled. The keys are documented in that
file's own comment block and published per refresh under the allowance's
"tunables".

The old VRAM admission hold stays as an alias of the renamed one, which now
holds on either leg, so the delegation tool needs no edit.

Requires patches 0003 and 0004. Note on ordering: patch 0011 squashes a
test-fixture correction that landed AFTER this change in fork history. The two
touch disjoint files, and the series was verified to apply in the printed
order.

BASE: the tree left by patch 0011 (see BASELINE.md).

PUBLISHED FORM. One absolute fleet log path in a comment becomes
<stack>/..., following ops/README.md.

