 # ===========================================================================
-# DEVICE VRAM PRESSURE — the axis that killed the lane  [bd-dre-i4qoy]
+# PREFILL CAPACITY — WHAT ACTUALLY BINDS CHILD CONCURRENCY  [bd-dre-i4qoy]
+# ===========================================================================
+# ⛔ 2026-09-30, GPU0 KV-pool cutover to host RAM. Pool 451,264 -> 1,310,720
+# tokens, KV VRAM 5.6 GB -> 1.26 GB, free VRAM 935-1,533 MiB -> 6,463 MiB. At the
+# same time the bounded-gather fix put prefill TRANSIENTS flat at 32 MiB at every
+# context length (was 1,024 MiB at 512K).
+#
+# Both of those broke the free-VRAM throttle below, in the same direction:
+#     affordable = 6,463 // 560 = 11 children, where it used to license ~1.
+# Andre's two mains were then measured at 11-17 tok/s each. Free device VRAM had
+# stopped being a capacity signal, because the thing it was a proxy FOR (prefill
+# transients, and the pool itself) had left the device.
+#
+# ⛔ WHAT WAS MEASURED ON THE LIVE LANE TONIGHT (the pool is NOT the constraint):
+#     pool used       187,840-417,664 / 1,310,720   (14-32%)
+#     running 3-4     waiting 2-4
+#     num_waiting_uncached_tokens  220,819-482,779   (up to 37% of the whole pool
+#                                                     sitting in the PREFILL queue)
+#     gen throughput  102-417 tok/s, nearly all of it spent on prefill
+# and, from the server's own cumulative counters at 23:54 UTC:
+#     total_prefill_uncached_tokens 7,535,276 / total_prefill_busy_us 888,433,873
+#       -> 8,481 prefill tokens/s   (vs gen_throughput 439 -> a 19:1 prefill:decode
+#          split, consistent with the 24:1-70:1 measured by the peer agent)
+#
+# The scarce resources, in the order they bind:
+#   1. PREFILL THROUGHPUT against --max-running-requests 4 and
+#      chunked_prefill_size 4,096. Every admitted request shares one 4,096-token
+#      prefill chunk budget per step, so a main's 300-500K prompt re-prefill is
+#      serialised behind whatever else is in flight.
+#   2. PREFIX-CACHE RESIDENCY. cache_hit_rate was 0.0 during the stall. A child
+#      with a distinct prompt evicts a main's cached prefix, and the main then
+#      pays its ENTIRE prompt again on its next turn. Measured by the peer agent:
+#      wasted prefill per call rises from 2,622 tokens with no contention to
+#      114,385 with five other in-flight requests.
+#   3. SCHEDULER SLOTS (max_running_requests 4). A main that cannot get a slot
+#      waits, however much pool and VRAM are free.
+#   4. POOL MEMORY. Last, at 14-32% used.
+#
+# ⛔ THE CONCURRENCY CURVE, RE-MEASURED [Andre 2026-09-30: "maybe we should cap
+# concurrency at where max tok/s are achieved so we can push things more"].
+# 6,436 `Decode batch` samples from the current build era of
+# <stack>/logs/qwen-flashnext-gpu0.log (2026-09-30 16:57 onward),
+# bucketed by #running-req, the first 180 s after each restart dropped, and
+# SEGMENTED by whether a prefill with >= 50,000 pending uncached tokens ran in the
+# preceding 10 s — because a post-restart prefill storm inflates the high-
+# concurrency buckets and was what made an earlier reading of this curve steeper
+# than it really is. The WARM segment is the one trusted here:
+#
+#   WARM (no cold prefill in flight)        COLD-contended (for contrast)
+#   conc    n   median agg   per-stream     conc    n   median agg   per-stream
+#     1  1060        176.5        176.5       1   933        160.6        160.6
+#     2   613        282.2        141.1       2  1085        273.1        136.5
+#     3   221        340.2        113.4       3   728        347.0        115.7
+#     4   128        409.6        102.4       4  1649        377.0         94.2
+#
+# ⛔ THERE IS NO INTERIOR AGGREGATE MAXIMUM below --max-running-requests 4.
+# Aggregate throughput rises monotonically (176 -> 282 -> 340 -> 410 warm) while a
+# single lane's own rate collapses (176 -> 141 -> 113 -> 102). So "cap at where max
+# tok/s is achieved" has no answer in aggregate terms: the cap is a deliberate
+# TRADE, and the objective that matters is A MAIN LANE'S OWN THROUGHPUT, because
+# the main lane is what Andre is reading at 22-28 tok/s.
+#
+# Share of its solo rate that a main lane retains, warm:
+#     conc 1 -> 100%      conc 2 -> 80.0%      conc 3 -> 64.2%      conc 4 -> 58.0%
+# TOTAL_STREAM_CAP = 2 is the largest concurrency at which a main lane keeps at
+# least two thirds of its solo decode rate; the third stream is what drops it
+# through that line (64.2%), for +21% aggregate. That is the derivation — not a
+# round number, and it is a runtime key (total_stream_cap), so Andre can push it
+# to 3 or 4 without relaunching a session once he wants the aggregate instead.
+#
+# ⛔ THE GATE, AND WHERE EVERY NUMBER COMES FROM.
+# Three conjunctive legs, each read live, none a round number:
+#
+# (A) SLOTS — two sub-legs, because one is an invariant and one is a preference.
+#     ⛔ INVARIANT (never traded away): a main's turn must NEVER queue behind a
+#        child. 2026-09-30, lane 226 was observed at "3 mine · 3/4 backend · 1
+#        free · 15 queued" — three of one session's OWN children holding three of
+#        four slots. So:
+#        hard_slot_cap  = max_running_requests - main_reserve - children_in_flight
+#        where main_reserve holds a slot for EVERY live main (at least one, even
+#        when the process scan finds none) plus whatever the mains have in flight.
+#     PREFERENCE (the throughput trade above):
+#        tuned_slot_cap = TOTAL_STREAM_CAP   - main_reserve - children_in_flight
+#     slot_cap = min(hard, tuned). At the live mrr=4 with one main and no children
+#     that is 1 — children take the SECOND slot and no more, which is the fix for
+#     the six-children-then-self-clamp behaviour: the gate refuses the second
+#     concurrent child outright instead of waiting for a 12-deep queue to teach it.
+#     With two mains the preference leg reaches 0 while the invariant leg still
+#     has room; that is deliberately NOT a refusal (see the state rules below),
+#     because blocking delegation outright whenever Andre has two sessions open
+#     would be a self-inflicted restriction, and three streams with two of them
+#     mains still queues no main.
+#
+# (B) PREFILL BACKLOG.  The backlog must drain inside the time the control plane
+#     can survive. That budget is not invented here: hermes_cli.goals
+#     DEFAULT_JUDGE_TIMEOUT is 30.0 s, and this module already records the
+#     measurement that made it matter — three identical small completions took
+#     28.5 s, 0.22 s, 0.22 s, the first simply waiting behind running requests,
+#     and "five of those in a row paused Andre's primary session". So:
+#         rate       = total_prefill_uncached_tokens / total_prefill_busy_us
+#                      (LIVE from /v1/loads; 8,481 tok/s measured tonight)
+#         budget     = rate * DRAIN_BUDGET_S          = 254,430 tokens
+#         backlog    = num_waiting_uncached_tokens
+#         headroom   = budget - backlog
+#     A backlog at or over the budget means the NEXT control-plane call already
+#     misses its timeout: headroom <= 0 -> admit nothing.
+#     Inside the headroom, each additional child is charged the EVICTION WASTE it
+#     causes, from the two measured anchors above: W(k) = 2,622 * g^k fitted
+#     through W(5) = 114,385, so g = (114,385/2,622)^(1/5) = 2.145 — per-call
+#     waste MULTIPLIES by 2.14 for every stream added. The admitted count is the
+#     largest k whose cumulative waste fits the headroom:
+#         W(1..5) = 5,624 / 12,065 / 25,880 / 55,513 / 119,076
+#         cum     = 5,624 / 17,689 / 43,569 / 99,082 / 218,158
+#     so an idle lane (backlog 0, headroom 254,430) affords 5 by this leg and is
+#     then held to 2 by leg (A) — and tonight's 482,779-token backlog affords 0.
+#     ⛔ The ceiling is deliberately NOT "as many as the headroom fits": the peer
+#     agent measured output saturating from mean concurrency ~1, with marginal
+#     return falling from 99 to 12 tok/s per added stream. Past a couple of
+#     streams we are buying 12 tok/s of child output with a 2.14x multiplier on
+#     every main's wasted prefill. Leg (A) is what makes that trade, on purpose.
+#
+# (C) POOL RESIDENCY (the bead's own wording: aggregate prompt tokens vs
+#     max_total_num_tokens). The backlog is prompt tokens that WILL land in the
+#     pool, so they are charged against it before children are:
+#         pool_cap = (pool - cache_reserve - used - backlog) // one_floor_child
+#     Tonight: (1,310,720 - 131,072 - 183,500 - 482,779) // 58,086 = 8 — this leg
+#     correctly does NOT bind, because the pool is not what is scarce.
+#
+# child_cap = min(A, B, C), and a main's window, growth ceiling and request
+# priority are untouched by all of it: this gate can only ever reduce CHILDREN.
+GPU0_PREFILL_RATE_TPS_DEFAULT = 8_481   # documented; the live counters override it
+PREFILL_RATE_TPS = 8_481
+# hermes_cli.goals.DEFAULT_JUDGE_TIMEOUT. Duplicated, not imported: goals imports
+# the agent package and a module-level cycle here would be worse than one number.
+# If that timeout is retuned, retune this with it (config key drain_budget_s).
+DRAIN_BUDGET_S = 30.0
+# The two measured anchors of the eviction-waste curve, and the growth factor
+# fitted through them. WASTE_GROWTH = (114385/2622)**(1/5) = 2.1448...
+WASTE_BASE_TOKENS = 2_622
+WASTE_GROWTH = 2.145
+WASTE_ANCHOR_HIGH_TOKENS = 114_385      # at 5 other in-flight requests
+# Slots held for each live main before any child is admitted. 1 = a main can
+# always get its turn's slot. Raising it to 2 also protects one auxiliary /
+# control-plane call per main (compaction, title, goal judge).
+MAIN_SLOT_RESERVE = 1
+# Total concurrent streams the backend is allowed to carry — mains INCLUDED.
+# Derived from the warm concurrency curve above: the largest concurrency at which
+# a main lane keeps >= 2/3 of its solo decode rate (80.0% at 2; 64.2% at 3).
+# Raise it (config key total_stream_cap) to buy aggregate throughput at a main
+# lane's expense: 3 -> +21% aggregate, main lane down to 64% of solo; 4 -> +45%
+# aggregate, main lane at 58%.
+TOTAL_STREAM_CAP = 2
+
+# ===========================================================================
+# DEVICE VRAM — NOW A BACKSTOP, NOT THE GATE  [bd-dre-i4qoy]
 # ===========================================================================
 # 2026-09-30 16:44:17: torch.OutOfMemoryError, "Tried to allocate 560.00 MiB ...
 # 280.94 MiB is free", during prefill with #queue-req 14 and #pending-token
@@ -253,22 +522,37 @@ CRITICAL_TOKEN_USAGE = 0.85
 #     watchdog's gpu0_newproc/vram_drop job and it stays there. It cannot detect
 #     "normal is already one allocation from death", which is what happened.
 #
-# ⛔ WHAT IS USED INSTEAD: CAPACITY, NOT ANOMALY.
-# Free VRAM is divided by the largest single allocation known to be fatal
-# (560 MiB, measured above) to get how many CONCURRENT PREFILLS the card can
-# absorb. SGLang chunks prefill at max_prefill_tokens=4,096, so that working set
-# is roughly per-in-flight-prefill and does not grow with total pending tokens —
-# what grows it is how many requests the scheduler admits to a prefill batch,
-# which is driven by QUEUE DEPTH. Hence the conjunction that was the real
-# pre-crash signature: affordable_prefills == 0 AND a deep queue.
-#     affordable = free_mib // PREFILL_ALLOC_MIB
-#     vram_child_cap = max(0, affordable - queued)
-# At the incident: 287 // 560 = 0 affordable, 14 queued -> cap 0, admit nothing.
-# At 0.97 with ~1,266 MiB free and an empty queue: 2 affordable -> 2 children.
-# This self-calibrates when Andre changes mem-fraction, because free VRAM is read
-# live rather than compared to a constant.
+# ⛔ WHAT THIS IS NOW, AND WHY IT IS NO LONGER THE PRIMARY GATE.
+# It used to be a capacity divisor: free_mib // 560 concurrent prefills, minus the
+# queue depth. That was sound while a prefill's transient VRAM was the scarce
+# thing. After tonight's cutover it is not: the KV pool is in host RAM and prefill
+# transients are FLAT at 32 MiB at every context length, so free VRAM no longer
+# moves with load — it just reads 6,463 MiB and licenses 11 children. Dividing a
